To overcome the limitations of traditional graph models, which often rely on information-poor initializations (e.g., random vectors), we employ the advanced protein language model ESM2 to generate deep semantic features for the sequence nodes. Through its internal, multi-layer Transformer architecture, ESM2 encodes an input amino acid sequence, generating a representation vector for each residue that is conditioned on its complete context within the sequence. As defined in Eq. (3), we obtain a single, fixed-dimension embedding for each peptide, \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
